{
	"nodes":[
		{"id":"30997b24d3f7f586","type":"group","x":-1700,"y":-300,"width":2340,"height":300,"color":"2","label":"Architektur-Prinzipien (SOLID)"},
		{"id":"02a750cf5497f77e","type":"group","x":1300,"y":-1000,"width":1420,"height":300,"color":"5","label":"Kapselung, Abstraktion & Information Hiding"},
		{"id":"4b9b1b156ebc4054","type":"group","x":-1700,"y":-1000,"width":960,"height":300,"color":"4","label":"Grundlagen"},
		{"id":"59c008ddc4b00c03","type":"group","x":-550,"y":-1000,"width":960,"height":300,"color":"6","label":"Vererbung & Polymorphie"},
		{"id":"ee89dcf03c869a1a","type":"group","x":-1700,"y":400,"width":960,"height":300,"color":"3","label":"Clean Code & Dependency Injection"},
		{"id":"59acf81e84cfd694","type":"group","x":-600,"y":400,"width":520,"height":300,"color":"1","label":"Python-Mechanik"},
		{"id":"fe92bb67387b1e89","type":"text","text":"# 1.1 OOP — Visuelle Übersicht\n\nGoldstandard professioneller Software-Entwicklung. Diese Canvas verknüpft [[1.1 OOP - Leitfaden|Leitfaden]] → [[1.1 OOP - Vertiefung|Vertiefung]] → [[1.1 OOP - Zusammenfassung|Zusammenfassung]] und zeigt zusätzlich, wie die vier `Vertiefung Plus/`-Notizen an die jeweiligen Konzeptcluster andocken.","x":-1200,"y":-1780,"width":1900,"height":240},
		{"id":"3fe10510ef8f435c","type":"file","file":"Documentation/Traineeship/Grundlagen der Software Entwicklung/1.1 OOP/1.1 OOP - Leitfaden.md","x":-1200,"y":-1450,"width":420,"height":180},
		{"id":"914a5ba5a65e6a56","type":"file","file":"Documentation/Traineeship/Grundlagen der Software Entwicklung/1.1 OOP/1.1 OOP - Vertiefung.md","x":-350,"y":-1450,"width":420,"height":180},
		{"id":"e5780cfcf56fa7a8","type":"file","file":"Documentation/Traineeship/Grundlagen der Software Entwicklung/1.1 OOP/1.1 OOP - Zusammenfassung.md","x":500,"y":-1450,"width":420,"height":180},
		{"id":"edceff026bc5aaeb","type":"text","text":"## Objektorientierung\n\nObjekt = **Zustand** + **Verhalten** in einer Einheit statt getrennter Daten/Funktionen (prozedural). Vorteil: natürliche Modellierung, Wartbarkeit, Wiederverwendbarkeit. In Python ist (fast) alles ein Objekt.","x":-1660,"y":-920,"width":420,"height":200,"color":"4"},
		{"id":"ea6dfa38fcd57f60","type":"text","text":"## Klassen\n\n**Klasse** = Bauplan, **Instanz** = konkretes Objekt. `__init__`/`self` setzen den Zustand. Falle: *veränderliche* Klassenattribute werden von allen Instanzen geteilt!","x":-1200,"y":-920,"width":420,"height":200,"color":"4"},
		{"id":"b5a815bb84308a14","type":"text","text":"## Vererbung\n\n„Ist-ein\"-Beziehung. `super()` vermeidet Duplikation, MRO entscheidet zur Laufzeit. **Favor composition over inheritance** bei „hat-ein\".","x":-510,"y":-920,"width":420,"height":200,"color":"6"},
		{"id":"1238b2ffdcb5865a","type":"file","file":"Documentation/Traineeship/Grundlagen der Software Entwicklung/1.1 OOP/Vertiefung Plus/Vererbung und Polymorphie - Tiefenperspektive.md","x":-550,"y":-650,"width":500,"height":140,"color":"6"},
		{"id":"abe1dfb62cedb589","type":"text","text":"## Polymorphie\n\nGleiche Schnittstelle, unterschiedliche Implementierung. **Nominal** (Vererbung) vs. **strukturell** (Duck Typing/Protocol). Operator Overloading zählt auch dazu.","x":-50,"y":-920,"width":420,"height":200,"color":"6"},
		{"id":"30168b05b3476127","type":"text","text":"## Kapselung\n\nZugriff auf Zustand kontrollieren. Python: nur Konventionen (`_x`, `__x`), kein echtes `private`. `@property`/`cached_property` als zentrales Werkzeug.","x":1340,"y":-920,"width":420,"height":200,"color":"5"},
		{"id":"ee7c9921af0819e4","type":"text","text":"## Abstraktion\n\nDesign-Entscheidung: **WAS** ist relevant? `abc`/`@abstractmethod` und `Protocol` setzen es technisch um. Abgrenzung zu Kapselung (WIE) ist Kontrollfrage!","x":1800,"y":-920,"width":420,"height":200,"color":"5"},
		{"id":"0c76704260c9c064","type":"text","text":"## Information Hiding\n\n**WARUM** wir abstrahieren/kapseln: Details verbergen, damit sich Code ändern lässt, ohne Aufrufer zu brechen. Gilt auch für Module/Pakete (`__all__`).","x":2260,"y":-920,"width":420,"height":200,"color":"5"},
		{"id":"cb7ba804208c7b19","type":"file","file":"Documentation/Traineeship/Grundlagen der Software Entwicklung/1.1 OOP/Vertiefung Plus/Kapselung - Properties, Descriptors und Slots.md","x":1300,"y":-650,"width":500,"height":140,"color":"5"},
		{"id":"be3f7050c450df5e","type":"text","text":"## Separation of Concerns\n\nUnterschiedliche Belange (Business-Logik/I/O) in getrennte Bausteine. Allgemeiner als SRP; ermöglicht saubere Testbarkeit.","x":-1660,"y":-220,"width":420,"height":200,"color":"2"},
		{"id":"ed3fd0e4b91bcc5d","type":"text","text":"## Single Responsibility\n\n„Ein Grund zur Änderung\" pro Klasse. **„Und\"-Test**, **God Object** als Warnsignal für Verletzung.","x":-1200,"y":-220,"width":420,"height":200,"color":"2"},
		{"id":"a0cb8b531b01a86d","type":"text","text":"## Loose Coupling / hohe Kohäsion\n\nKopplung = Abhängigkeit *zwischen* Klassen (lose!), Kohäsion = Zusammengehörigkeit *innerhalb* einer Klasse (hoch!).","x":-740,"y":-220,"width":420,"height":200,"color":"2"},
		{"id":"afeb39195f150617","type":"text","text":"## Open/Closed\n\nOffen für Erweiterung, geschlossen für Veränderung. Wachsende `if isinstance()`-Ketten verletzen es — Polymorphie löst es auf.","x":-280,"y":-220,"width":420,"height":200,"color":"2"},
		{"id":"6b4ea2161094e08d","type":"text","text":"## SOLID (S-O-L-I-D)\n\nSRP, Open/Closed, **Liskov Substitution**, **Interface Segregation**, Dependency Inversion — fünf sich verstärkende Design-Prinzipien.","x":180,"y":-220,"width":420,"height":200,"color":"2"},
		{"id":"93264f1ccbb3b337","type":"file","file":"Documentation/Traineeship/Grundlagen der Software Entwicklung/1.1 OOP/Vertiefung Plus/SOLID-Prinzipien.md","x":-1700,"y":50,"width":500,"height":140,"color":"2"},
		{"id":"35bbbed914271e27","type":"text","text":"## Clean Code\n\nCode wird öfter gelesen als geschrieben. Dachbegriff über sprechende Namen, DRY, kleine Funktionen, keine Magic Numbers — bündelt SRP/SoC/Kapselung/Open-Closed.","x":-1660,"y":480,"width":420,"height":200,"color":"3"},
		{"id":"d1dac75b6215c3db","type":"text","text":"## Dependency Injection\n\nAbhängigkeiten werden **hereingereicht** statt selbst erzeugt (Constructor Injection). Löst Testbarkeit + lose Kopplung. DI ≠ DIP (Technik vs. Prinzip).","x":-1200,"y":480,"width":420,"height":200,"color":"3"},
		{"id":"cd441770bd53d3ba","type":"file","file":"Documentation/Traineeship/Grundlagen der Software Entwicklung/1.1 OOP/Vertiefung Plus/Dependency Injection und Inversion of Control.md","x":-1700,"y":750,"width":500,"height":140,"color":"3"},
		{"id":"9e064a9e4ef094b5","type":"text","text":"## dunder Methoden & Dataclasses\n\n`__len__`/`__iter__`/`__eq__` steuern eingebautes Verhalten. `@dataclass` generiert Boilerplate; `field(default_factory=...)` gegen mutable Defaults.","x":-560,"y":480,"width":440,"height":200,"color":"1"}
	],
